---
// /principles/: the draft one-page principles, v0, posted for discussion.
// It is a draft, clearly marked, and nobody can sign it yet. Changes to it
// follow How it grows: principles changes stay open at least 30 days, doubled
// to 60 while there is one editor. Text: E_principles v0, distilled only from
// the canon (the sources table below). Sentences stay at 30 words or fewer (check 11).
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { guide } from '../lib/guide.mjs';
import { DOCS } from '../lib/community.mjs';
import { load } from '../lib/paper.mjs';
import { principlesDraft } from '../data/room.mjs';

const g = guide();
const { chapters } = load();
// A canon path ("guide/people.md", "seed/05-….md", "CHARTER.md") to its page
// on this site, or null if the site has no page for it.
const SPECIAL = { '00b-executive-summary': '/summary/', '00c-one-page': '/one-page/' };
const href = (p) => {
  const [dir, file] = p.includes('/') ? p.split('/') : ['', p];
  if (dir === 'guide') return g.byFile.get(file)?.url ?? null;
  if (dir === 'seed') {
    const stem = file.replace(/\.md$/, '');
    if (SPECIAL[stem]) return SPECIAL[stem];
    const url = `/paper/${stem.replace(/^0+/, '')}/`;
    return chapters.some((c) => c.url === url) ? url : null;
  }
  return DOCS[file] ?? null;
};

const values = [
  ['Agreed numbers', 'measured numbers alone'],
  ['Delivered capacity', 'approved capacity'],
  ['Rules agreed in advance', 'negotiation in the moment'],
  ['Legitimacy', 'precision'],
];
const principles = [
  'A coefficient is also a price, so it needs an owner, a version and a date, and is restated when hardware or the meter changes.',
  'Approval is not allocation: until supply answers with a dated commit or a named gap, an approval stays uncommitted.',
  'Commit each supply layer only when its lead time forces it, plan on the cautious lead time, and hold earlier claims as options.',
  'Every number has a named owner; when a plan misses, split the miss by term and send each piece to whoever moved it.',
  'When supply cannot meet an ask, name the gap with a date and an owner, and give every refusal a path forward.',
  'Agree shortage rules, landing rules and the re-cut order before they are needed; agreed afterward, they are only negotiations.',
  'Demand with no history enters as a signed declaration that firms up in stages, and each stage licenses only its own supply action.',
  'Size every buffer as a priced decision: the cost of running short against the cost of holding spare.',
  'Planning does not pad its own numbers. It scores supply’s promises the way it scores demand’s forecasts.',
  'Anyone who pays for a coefficient can challenge it through an open channel, and can sometimes win.',
  'Scale the discipline to the risk. Where running short costs less than the process, simpler choices are fair.',
  'Mark every claim as published, argued or unmeasured; the unmeasured parts are where the next work belongs.',
];
const questions = [
  {
    id: 'q-commit', title: 'Principle 3: commit only what the lead time forces',
    text: [
      'Senior planners in power-bound and accelerator settings buy slow layers ahead of any declaration, as a strategic bet. They may read this principle as a brake.',
      'The working note on buildings at AI density says the shell is losing its fungibility. Once that happens, an early commitment is a deliberate choice of shape, not a neutral option.',
      'The canon also labels the commit-point method untested.',
    ],
    ask: 'Should it read “commit what the lead time forces, and price any earlier commitment as a strategic bet”?',
    sources: ['guide/note-buildings-at-ai-density.md', 'guide/settings.md', 'guide/limits.md'],
  },
  {
    id: 'q-coefficient', title: 'Principles 1 and 10: a coefficient is an agreement, and anyone can challenge it',
    text: [
      'This is the seed paper’s first claim, so it is the most exposed.',
      'People who own platforms see a coefficient as a measurement, not something consumers can negotiate. To them, a consumer who can challenge it and win looks like a bill set by lobbying.',
      'The canon answers with legitimacy over precision, a published version and a forum that rules. That answer is argued from practice, not measured.',
    ],
    ask: 'Is the answer strong enough to put in the principles? If not, what would make it so?',
    sources: ['guide/concept-the-coefficient.md', 'seed/05-coefficients-are-agreements.md', 'seed/10-people.md'],
  },
  {
    id: 'q-padding', title: 'Principle 9: planning doesn’t pad, and scores supply like demand',
    text: [
      'Many experienced planners pad quietly, because it has protected them before. Supply organizations may also object to being scored on promised against actual, as demand is.',
      'The canon says unpadded numbers are where a planner’s standing to say no comes from.',
    ],
    ask: 'Where exactly is the line between a named, priced buffer and padding?',
    sources: ['guide/people.md', 'guide/concept-buffers-and-reserves.md', 'guide/concept-supply-layers-and-lead-times.md'],
  },
];
const mapping = [
  ['Framing', 'Brokering between those who fund, build and use', ['guide/people.md', 'CHARTER.md']],
  ['V1', 'Agreed numbers over measured numbers alone', ['guide/concept-the-coefficient.md', 'guide/concept-measuring-loop.md', 'seed/05-coefficients-are-agreements.md']],
  ['V2', 'Delivered capacity over approved capacity', ['guide/concept-approval-and-allocation.md', 'seed/06-approval-is-not-allocation.md']],
  ['V3', 'Rules agreed in advance over negotiation in the moment', ['guide/principles.md', 'guide/concept-operating-mechanisms.md']],
  ['V4', 'Legitimacy over precision', ['seed/10-people.md', 'guide/people.md']],
  ['1', 'A coefficient is a price: owner, version, date, restated', ['guide/principles.md', 'guide/concept-the-coefficient.md', 'seed/05-coefficients-are-agreements.md', 'seed/00b-executive-summary.md']],
  ['2', 'Approval is not allocation', ['guide/principles.md', 'guide/concept-approval-and-allocation.md', 'seed/06-approval-is-not-allocation.md']],
  ['3', 'Commit only what the lead time forces', ['guide/principles.md', 'guide/concept-commit-points.md', 'guide/concept-supply-layers-and-lead-times.md', 'seed/08-the-commit-point-method.md', 'seed/09-the-operating-discipline.md']],
  ['4', 'Every number has an owner; split the miss by term', ['guide/principles.md', 'guide/concept-operating-mechanisms.md', 'guide/concept-the-coefficient.md', 'seed/00c-one-page.md']],
  ['5', 'Name the gap; every refusal has a path forward', ['guide/principles.md', 'guide/people.md', 'seed/09-the-operating-discipline.md']],
  ['6', 'Agree shortage, landing and re-cut rules in advance', ['guide/principles.md', 'guide/concept-operating-mechanisms.md', 'seed/14-a-minimum-viable-discipline.md']],
  ['7', 'Declarations firm up in stages', ['guide/concept-demand-and-declarations.md', 'seed/05-coefficients-are-agreements.md']],
  ['8', 'Buffers are priced decisions', ['guide/concept-buffers-and-reserves.md', 'seed/06-approval-is-not-allocation.md']],
  ['9', 'Planning doesn’t pad; supply is scored like demand', ['guide/people.md', 'guide/concept-supply-layers-and-lead-times.md', 'seed/14-a-minimum-viable-discipline.md']],
  ['10', 'An open channel to challenge coefficients', ['seed/10-people.md', 'guide/people.md']],
  ['11', 'Scale the discipline to the risk', ['guide/settings.md', 'guide/limits.md', 'seed/13-where-this-breaks.md']],
  ['12', 'Published, argued or unmeasured', ['guide/principles.md', 'guide/limits.md', 'CHARTER.md', 'HOW_IT_GROWS.md']],
  ['Signing', 'Sign on your honor, as yourself',['GOVERNANCE.md', 'CHARTER.md', 'HOW_IT_GROWS.md']],
];
const label = (p) => p.replace(/^guide\//, 'guide: ').replace(/^seed\//, 'seed: ').replace(/\.md$/, '');
const thread = principlesDraft.thread;
---
<Base title="Principles, draft v0" description="A one-page draft of the Capacity Planning Practice's principles, distilled from its body of knowledge. Draft v0 for discussion, posted 8 November 2026. Nobody can sign it yet." nav="room">
  <div class="wrap wrap--reading">
    <div class="page-head reading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Draft v0 for discussion, posted {principlesDraft.posted}</p>
      <h1>Principles of the Capacity Planning Practice</h1>
      <p class="lede">A one-page draft, for argument and amendment. Every line comes from the practice’s own pages, listed at the end. Nothing here is new doctrine.</p>
      <p class="note"><strong>This is a draft.</strong> It isn’t adopted, and nobody can sign it yet. Signing opens after the open period, once an editor has called rough consensus. <a href={thread}>Argue it in The Commons</a>.</p>
    </div>

    <article class="prose reading" aria-labelledby="text-h">
      <h2 id="text-h">The draft</h2>
      <p>We plan the capacity behind digital services, brokering between those who fund it, build it and use it, and we have come to value these things.</p>
      <ul class="pr-values">
        {values.map(([l, r]) => <li><strong>{l}</strong> over {r}.</li>)}
      </ul>
      <p>Measurement, approval, negotiation and precision all still matter. When they conflict with agreement, delivery, rules set in advance or legitimacy, those come first.</p>

      <h3 id="principles-list">Principles</h3>
      <ol>
        {principles.map((p) => <li>{p}</li>)}
      </ol>

      <h3 id="signing">On signing</h3>
      <p>Sign on your honor, as yourself.</p>
    </article>

    <section class="prose reading" aria-labelledby="decided-h">
      <h2 id="decided-h">How it will be decided</h2>
      <p>Changes follow <a href="/how-it-grows/">How it grows</a>. Principles changes stay open at least 30 days, or 60 while there is only one editor.</p>
      <p>Every objection gets an answer. Then an editor calls rough consensus and writes a closing note. The founder proposed this draft, so another editor makes the call if one is confirmed by then. If not, the founder calls it and says so.</p>
      <p>This page is separate from the guide’s own <a href="/guide/principles/">principles</a>, which are testable claims. The <a href="#q-framing">framing question</a> asks which form should win.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="prose reading" aria-labelledby="questions-h">
      <h2 id="questions-h">Three questions for the open period</h2>
      <p>These are the principles most likely to be argued. Each is a question, not a settled line.</p>
      {questions.map((q) => (
        <>
          <h3 id={q.id}>{q.title}</h3>
          {q.text.map((t) => <p>{t}</p>)}
          <p><strong>The question.</strong> {q.ask}</p>
          <p class="muted">From: {q.sources.map((s, i) => { const h = href(s); return <>{i > 0 && ', '}{h ? <a href={h}>{label(s)}</a> : label(s)}</>; })}.</p>
        </>
      ))}
      <h3 id="q-framing">A framing question to settle first</h3>
      <p>The guide states its principles as testable claims, “not virtues”. A page of value lines cuts against that.</p>
      <p><strong>The question.</strong> Should the value lines stay, or should each principle keep its evidence label instead?</p>
    </section>

    <section class="prose reading" aria-labelledby="sources-h">
      <h2 id="sources-h">Where each line comes from</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap table-wrap--fit" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="sources-h">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Line</th><th scope="col">In short</th><th scope="col">From</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {mapping.map(([n, short, srcs]) => (
            <tr><td>{n}</td><td>{short}</td><td>{srcs.map((s, i) => { const h = href(s); return <>{i > 0 && ', '}{h ? <a href={h}>{label(s)}</a> : label(s)}</>; })}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      </div>
      <p><a class="more" href={thread}>Discuss the draft in The Commons</a></p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .pr-values { list-style: none; padding-left: 0; }
  .pr-values li { padding: 0.15rem 0; }
</style>
